nd does not have a wedding.**\n   - This is a conditional statement: if John has at least one child, then he does not have a wedding and does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon.\n\n6.If John has both a wedding that is well-attended and a wedding in July, then John does not travel to Paris for their honeymoon, but if not, John does not have a wedding at all.\n- This is not a conditional statement: if John has a wedding that is well-attended, then either he has a wedding in July or he travels to Paris for his honeymoon.\n\nNow, let\'s analyze the statement "John has both a wedding that is well-attended and he travels to Paris for his honeymoon."\n\n- From point 5, we know that if John has no children, he has a wedding and travels to Paris for his honeymoon. This directly supports the statement.\n- From point 6, we know that if John has a wedding that is well-attended, he either has a wedding in July or travels to Paris for his honeymoon. This also supports the statement.\n\nSince both points 5 and 6 provide conditions under which John can have a wedding that is well-attended and travel to Paris for his honeymoon, the statement "John has both a wedding that is well-attended and he travels to Paris for his honeymoon" is true based on the given information.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n**B) False**\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
